--- title
# LOGV Learn — Education Platform Architecture

--- body
Course authoring, learner progress and commercial enrollment.

--- actions
[Back to projects](/en/#products) · [Português](/projects/pt/logv-learn/)

:::

::: section architecture
## Architecture overview

LOGV Learn organizes teaching content, learner experiences and course access in a web application. Its public architecture can be described through four domains:

- **Content management:** courses, modules, lessons and authoring workflows.
- **Learning experience:** enrollment, playback, study progress and learner interaction.
- **Identity and authorization:** accounts and access to learner and administrative experiences.
- **Commercial lifecycle:** purchase initiation, payment confirmation and corresponding course access.

These flows share application persistence while requiring separate business rules and validation.
:::

::: section engineering
## Design decisions and trade-offs

A cohesive application makes it easier for course and learner experiences to share state. Payment events, however, arrive asynchronously and must be reconciled with enrollment. Identity, entitlement and content navigation remain distinct conceptual responsibilities even if deployed together.
:::

::: section alternatives
## Alternative architectures

A hosted learning management system reduces product development and operations but limits customization. Independent payment and entitlement services increase isolation but expand the number of systems to operate. A modular monolith offers a practical progression if boundaries remain explicit and testable.
:::

::: section verification
## Quality strategy and evidence

The codebase contains education and payment-related application flows, but no comprehensive automated test suite was established in this review. This case study therefore describes intended validation practices rather than claiming passing acceptance tests.

Coverage should include course publication, access rules, progress continuity, payment-state transitions, cancellations, duplicate notifications and usability. Detailed financial processing, provider credentials and any identified implementation defects remain restricted to private engineering records.
:::

::: section constraints
## Constraints and evolution

Educational content must remain accessible and consistent across browsers, while paid access depends on reliable asynchronous processes. Privacy, accessibility, operational support and clear learner-facing recovery are important parts of the architecture. Internal operational specifics are intentionally omitted.
:::

